{"parentUuid":"b7d52801-a27f-4fb9-ac7c-5c1107c29634","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"b0530d39-334c-4d81-b949-0367cf262803","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"robust-napping-matsumoto","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01PVejdvJ9DgjVxsrh3BCG2D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The answer has been written to `/root/answer.txt`.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Answer: 25**\n\nHere's how I solved it:\n\n1. **Data**: The Excel file contains 6,000 turns (with turn 15 missing, leaving 5,999) across 3,000 games. Each turn has 6 dice rolls.\n\n2. **Scoring**: For each turn, I computed scores for all 6 categories:\n   - **High and Often**: highest value × count of that value\n   - **Summation**: sum of all 6 dice\n   - **Highs and Lows**: highest × lowest × (highest - lowest)\n   - **Only Two Numbers**: 30 points if exactly 2 distinct values\n   - **All the Numbers**: 40 points if all of {1,2,3,4,5,6} present\n   - **Ordered Four**: 50 points if 4 consecutive increasing/decreasing numbers appear contiguously\n\n3. **Game scoring**: For each game (2 turns), I found the optimal assignment of different categories to each turn to maximize the combined score.\n\n4. **Player matching**: Odd-numbered games → Player 1, even-numbered games → Player 2. Matched as (game 1 vs game 2), (game 3 vs game 4), etc. — yielding 1,500 matches.\n\n5. **Results**: Player 1 won 740 matches, Player 2 won 715 matches, with 45 ties. The difference is **740 - 715 = 25**."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":243,"cache_read_input_tokens":60245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":243,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtCWDKJgpntnpGoNSt6k","type":"assistant","uuid":"ec1d8cad-745c-4f04-b8fb-cd4ce60a59ae","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:01:02.543Z"}
